Context. This page belongs to the combinatorial description of the boundary strata of the Deligne–Mumford compactification of M_{g,ν}. A stratum is indexed by a 'ν-graph' G = (S, Ã, σ_Ã, ∂_G; I → S, g: S → N). Here:
- S is the set of vertices (components of the stable curve);
- Ã is the set of oriented edges, i.e. half-edges;
- σ_Ã is a fixed-point-free involution;
- ∂ = o: Ã → S is the origin map;
- I → S places the ν marked points;
- g gives the genus of each component.

The previous page defined 'simplismes de simplification' between ν-graphs. These are injective subgraph inclusions together with an injection of legs I' → I ⊔ J̃, compatible with genera. This page explains how the data of a ν-graph, consisting of the two sets Ã(G) and I(G) each with an 'origin' map to S(G), is encoded by one generalised notion: 'graphes avec bandes aplaties' (graphs with flattened bands, i.e. with free ends).

Topological definition. Consider pairs (X, S) with X compact and S ⊂ X finite, such that:
(a) X is a star (cone on finitely many branches) near each point of S;
(b) each connected component of X∖S is a non-compact 1-manifold with boundary.
Such a component is therefore either an open interval with both ends on S, or a half-open interval [0,1) whose boundary point is a free end lying outside S. The aim is that every closure of an edge 'has a vertex'.

Equivalent formulation. X is a topological 1-complex and S is a finite subset containing all vertices of order ≥ 3, such that no component of X∖S is compact. Equivalently, every component of X meets S, i.e. S → π_0(X) is surjective.

Combinatorial classification. Such pairs are classified by systems (S, Î, σ_Î, o_Î):
- S and Î are finite sets;
- σ_Î is an involution of Î, now allowed to have fixed points;
- o_Î: Î → S is a map.
The next page makes the dictionary explicit. Fixed points I = Î^σ are the flattened bands, i.e. the boundary points of X not in S, i.e. the legs. The complement Ã = Î∖I with the restricted involution is an ordinary graph. So a graph with flattened bands is the same as an ordinary graph (S, Ã, σ, o) plus a set I with a map I → S.

This is exactly the now-standard 'flags/half-edges with involution, legs = fixed points' formalism for stable graphs of pointed curves. Here it is given together with its topological realisation as a compact 1-complex with marked vertex set and free endpoints.
